Looking at the mutation: original `if(!cb)` vs mutated `if(false)`. When `find(test, cb)` is called with both args, `!cb` is false, so the else branch runs: `test = prop(test) || id`. When mutated, `if(false)` means the else branch always runs too — same behavior. So the difference only shows when `find` is called with a single argument (just a callback, no test). In the original, `cb = test, test = id` — cb becomes the passed function, test becomes id. In the mutated version, `if(false)` means the else branch runs: `test = prop(test) || id`. Here `test` is the passed function (which is actually the callback), and `prop(fn)` returns a function that accesses `data[fn]` — not the identity. So the sink would try to access `data[callbackFn]` which is undefined, and never match.

So the test should call `find(cb)` with a single argument.

